# ===========================================================================
# Swift deploy env — copy to deploy/.env and fill in. Grouped by whether a
# value is REQUIRED to boot, or OPTIONAL (a safe default / mock is used until
# you wire the real provider). Compose supplies the database parts, REDIS_URL
# and MEILISEARCH_URL — you do NOT set those here — and the app assembles
# DATABASE_URL from them.
#
# SECRETS DO NOT GO IN THIS FILE. Every credential lives in the encrypted host
# store (deploy/swift-secrets; `sudo swift-secrets set NAME` reads the value on
# stdin) and reaches the containers as a file on tmpfs, wired as
# NAME_FILE=/run/secrets/NAME. The generated ones are wired in
# docker-compose.yml; a provider's secret is wired HERE, next to its switch,
# when the provider is turned on (the commented NAME_FILE lines below).
# pilot-up.sh refuses a filled-in .env that declares a secret.
#
#   cp .env.deploy.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# NEVER commit the filled-in .env. Rotate any secret that touches a real
# provider before go-live (see deploy/README.md "Production").
# ===========================================================================

# ---- REQUIRED settings (the secrets themselves are in the encrypted store) ---
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD, MEILISEARCH_KEY, JWT_SECRET, OTP_HASH_SECRET, MASTER_KEK,
# STORAGE_SIGNING_SECRET, CONSENT_IP_PEPPER, ATTRIB_SALT, IDENTITY_SALT,
# SCAN_IP_SALT, ADS_EVENT_SECRET, TEST_CONTROL_SECRET, METRICS_TOKEN and
# HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN are generated by ./deploy/gen-secrets.sh straight into
# the store and wired in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Other store-held secrets, wired HERE when you set one (`sudo swift-secrets
# set NAME`, then uncomment). The two keyed-hash secrets fall back to
# JWT_SECRET; the bootstrap password (16+ characters) provisions the first
# operator login; the webhook secret comes with MMG onboarding.
# SERVICE_PROVIDER_CURSOR_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/SERVICE_PROVIDER_CURSOR_SECRET
# VELOCITY_KEY_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/VELOCITY_KEY_SECRET
# SWIFT_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/SWIFT_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD
# AGENT_CASH_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/AGENT_CASH_WEBHOOK_SECRET
# The compliance watch's outbound alert webhook (its token is in the URL):
# CW_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/CW_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL
# Browser origins allowed by the API (comma-separated as supported by the app).
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# ---- Host wiring (safe defaults; override for your box) --------------------
# pilot-up.sh refuses any other target. API_HOST is the DNS name only, without
# scheme or path; Caddy obtains and renews its TLS certificate automatically.
PILOT_ENV=staging
API_HOST=
LOG_LEVEL=info
# [DEP-2] Who may set X-Forwarded-For. This was `1` (a hop count); fastify now
# FAILS hop-count trust CLOSED — a direct client can defeat hop counting by
# supplying enough hops — so a bare number is refused at boot. Name the proxy
# instead. The API publishes a host port, so a reverse proxy on the host reaches
# it from Docker's bridge gateway; `loopback` covers a proxy sharing the network
# namespace. Use `true` ONLY if every path to this service goes through a proxy
# you control and the port is not reachable directly.
TRUST_PROXY=loopback, 172.16.0.0/12
# Staging with the mock providers below runs in development mode. The app's
# production guard REFUSES mock KYC/MMG/card rails when NODE_ENV=production
# (a deliberate safety fail-close). Set NODE_ENV=production ONLY after every
# provider in this file is a real credential — that is the LB-010 launch gate.
# [TA-S1-007] The value must be EXACTLY production | development | test |
# loadtest. Unset or misspelled ("prod", "Production") is not "development":
# the compose file refuses to start and the API refuses to boot, because a
# quiet development posture on a live host is the worst possible default.
NODE_ENV=development

# ---- Providers: default to SAFE mocks; wire the real ones for launch -------
# Each of these has an honest built-in default so staging runs end-to-end
# WITHOUT external accounts. Every one of them is an LB-010 launch item — the
# real credentials are a FOUNDER / third-party deliverable, not code.

# Maps/geocoding: 'haversine' needs no key and is deterministic. Switch to
# 'osrm' (self-host, free) or 'google' (needs GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY_BACKEND) for
# real road ETAs. See deploy/docker-compose.osrm.yml to self-host OSRM.
# [C2/S3] osrm is the launch standard — every fare, ETA and dispatch ranking
# inherits this line. haversine is a dev-only fallback; deploying it mis-states
# all three at once in Georgetown. Watch osrmOutcomeCounter for fallback rate.
MAPS_PROVIDER=osrm
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY_BACKEND_FILE=/run/secrets/GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY_BACKEND
OSRM_URL=http://osrm:5000

# Object storage for uploads (verification docs, photos). 'local' writes to a
# volume (fine for staging); 's3' needs the AWS_* vars for launch.
STORAGE_PROVIDER=local
STORAGE_ALLOW_LOCAL=1
# The keys: `sudo swift-secrets set AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` (and the secret), then
# uncomment the wiring. The backup unit reads the same two from the store.
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID_FILE=/run/secrets/AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# AWS_REGION=auto
# AWS_S3_ENDPOINT=
# [REPORT-036 F036-04] The variable the code actually reads. The template used
# to say S3_BUCKET=, which NOTHING consumes — anyone following it with
# STORAGE_PROVIDER=s3|r2 hit a hard construction throw at boot.
# AWS_S3_BUCKET=
# [D-4] Version the object bucket: `aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket <AWS_S3_BUCKET>
# --endpoint-url <AWS_S3_ENDPOINT> --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled`. The preflight
# asks the bucket and fails the documents check until it answers Enabled — a deleted or
# overwritten KYC document is otherwise gone, and no database dump brings it back.

# ── OFFSITE BACKUPS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bucket deploy/backup.sh uploads each database dump to, using the S3
# credentials above. WITHOUT THIS, dumps stay on the machine they back up —
# one disk failure loses the database and every backup of it.
#
# Use a SEPARATE bucket from AWS_S3_BUCKET: a dump is every user's row, and it
# wants different retention and different access from customer uploads.
# The daily freshness job pages while this is unset, which is deliberate.
# BACKUP_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_PREFIX=db
# BACKUP_RETAIN_DAYS=14
# Dead-box detection: a healthchecks.io check URL. backup.sh pings it after a
# VERIFIED backup; the external service alarms on silence — the one failure the
# in-box alarms cannot report is the box itself dying.
# The check URL's path token is a ping capability only — it can say "the backup
# ran", never read anything — so it stays a plain setting here; rotate it at the
# checks service if it ever leaks.
# BACKUP_HEARTBEAT_URL=
# [D-4] MASTER_KEK escrow. gen-secrets.sh prints the fingerprint (sha256 of the key bytes)
# beside the key; record it here once a copy of the key is held OFF this machine. The
# preflight recomputes it from the live MASTER_KEK: a mismatch means the key was rotated and
# the escrow was not — the failure that leaves every restored document as ciphertext.
# MASTER_KEK_ESCROW_FINGERPRINT=

# Push notifications: 'expo' uses Expo's free push service (creds attach to the
# EAS build, not here). 'dev' logs instead of sending; offers still work over sockets.
PUSH_PROVIDER=expo                # REQUIRED in production. 'dev' is in-memory and swallows every push while reporting success — the boot guard refuses to start on it
NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=dev
# For NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=twilio: the three identifiers here (value-free in
# this example), the API key secret in the store, wired by the *_FILE line.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_API_KEY_SID=
# TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET
TWILIO_FROM=
# TWILIO_WEBHOOK_AUTH_TOKEN=  # future inbound webhook signature verification only

# Email (EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp): SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_TLS, SMTP_USER and
# EMAIL_FROM here; the mailbox password in the store, wired by the *_FILE line.
# SMTP_PASS_FILE=/run/secrets/SMTP_PASS

# KYC (identity verification): 'sandbox' auto-approves in staging. Real providers
# (didit / id-analyzer) need their API keys and are an LB-017 launch item.
KYC_PROVIDER=sandbox

# MMG (Guyana mobile money): the checkout pay-link rail. 'sandbox' issues a fake
# link in staging. The REAL merchant credentials are the LB-010 money rail and
# MUST be set before taking a single real payment. Production also REJECTS the
# sandbox driver (SPS-F-0011).
MMG_DRIVER=sandbox
# MMG_API_URL=
# MMG_MERCHANT_ID=
# MMG_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/MMG_API_KEY
# MMG_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/MMG_PASSWORD
# MMG_MKEY_FILE=/run/secrets/MMG_MKEY
# MMG_MSECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/MMG_MSECRET
# MMG_PAY_URL_ALLOWED_HOSTS=mmg.gy,pay.mmg.gy

# Cards are OFF for the pilot: both settings below are required. Disabled
# enrollment, charges and refunds return CARD_RAIL_DISABLED without a gateway.
# Existing uncertain charges stay unresolved until a live provider is restored.
# MMG production requirements above are unchanged. To enable card billing,
# choose stripe or powertranz and supply its full production credentials;
# sandbox, unset and misspelled providers are refused in production.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER=disabled
CARD_RAIL_KILL=1
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/PAYMENT_GATEWAY_KEY
# PAYMENT_GATEWAY_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/PAYMENT_GATEWAY_SECRET
# POWERTRANZ_API_URL=

# ---- Product flags (match the current launch posture) ----------------------
LIFECYCLE_V2=1            # server-timed checkout hold / free-cancel window
ORDER_HOLD_MINUTES=5      # [B1/S4] the designed five-minute hold (Total Audit build order)
VENDOR_RESPONSE_SLA_MINUTES=10
DISPATCH_AVAILABILITY=1

# ---- The upkeep organs (each inert until its value exists) -----------------
# Error tracking: @sentry/node is installed, initialized and tested — this one
# value turns it on. Free tier at sentry.io; GlitchTip (self-hosted) speaks the
# same DSN if you ever want off their cloud. Unset = clean no-op.
# SENTRY_DSN_FILE=/run/secrets/SENTRY_DSN
# No model provider key belongs here. Swift contains no AI runtime, and a source
# scan (no-ai-boundary.test.ts) fails the build if one reappears in application
# code. Blocking network egress to provider hosts is separate and not yet done.

# ── Tenant wall [REPORT-111 P0.3] ──
# The API refuses to start in production when the database tenant wall does not
# bind its connection. Until the least-privilege login exists, that is the
# posture — so this must be set, and it is a DECLARATION, not a switch: it buys
# exactly one tenant, and the second refuses to start regardless.
# [TEN-01] When the least-privilege application login exists, audited system
# work runs on the swift_bypass_rls login's own URL — the most privileged
# database credential, so it is store-held and file-delivered, never written
# here. Until then it stays unset and the declaration below carries the wall.
# SYSTEM_DATABASE_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/SYSTEM_DATABASE_URL
TENANT_WALL_EXPAND_ATTESTED=1

# ── Database connection pool [P1 · WS-8.3] ──
# The saturation alarm (POOL_WAIT_ALERT_THRESHOLD) tells an operator to "raise
# connection_limit". These are the dials it means. They were documented only in
# apps/api/.env.example, so an operator following THIS template never saw them
# [R037-07].
#
# ⚠️ THESE ARE A SHARED BUDGET against Postgres `max_connections` (default 100).
# Every API replica takes its own pool, and migrations, psql, Studio, backups
# and analytics all need slots on top. Size the total, not each process alone:
#   (api_replicas x DB_POOL_SIZE_API) + DB_POOL_SIZE_WORKER + tools + reserve
# must stay comfortably under max_connections. At the defaults, five API
# replicas plus the worker already reach 100.
#
# With RUN_WORKERS unset this compose runs ONE combined process (HTTP + all 19
# job consumers on one client), which uses DB_POOL_SIZE_COMBINED.
DB_POOL_SIZE_COMBINED=40    # combined API+worker process (the default topology)
DB_POOL_SIZE_API=15         # HTTP-only process (RUN_WORKERS=0)
DB_POOL_SIZE_WORKER=25      # the dedicated `node dist/worker.js` process
DB_POOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10  # seconds a query waits for a free connection; 0 = forever
